From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tseng <tseng.jayj@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 15:26:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] daemon: make genesis and runtime network defaults
 self-contained

An ordinary build of this daemon could construct a different genesis than an
identical binary next to it, and on an ordinary start it reached for another
project's infrastructure. Both are fixed here. No consensus value changes.

GENESIS IS NO LONGER ENVIRONMENT-DEPENDENT

generate_genesis_block() read MEEPCOIN_EXPERIMENTAL_GENESIS_TS and used it as
the genesis timestamp. That function is shared production code: the daemon
calls it from Blockchain::init() when initializing an empty database, and
wallet2 calls it when deriving the network genesis. Two copies of the SAME
binary therefore produced DIFFERENT genesis blocks -- different timestamp,
hashing blob and block id -- purely because one inherited an environment
variable. The override is removed with no replacement; a runtime flag would
have the identical defect. src/meepcoin_genesis/HARNESS_COMPAT.md records that
the old Python measurement launchers depend on the removed hook and are NO-GO
until separately redesigned. Those scripts were deliberately not edited.

NO INHERITED DEFAULT PEER

Removed Monero's four clearnet DNS seed hostnames, sixteen fallback IP seeds on
ports 18080/28080/38080, six onion seeds and three I2P seeds. Those are Monero
nodes; MeepCoin cannot join Monero's chain, so contacting them only discloses
this node to a foreign network's operators. No MeepCoin bootstrap authority has
been reviewed and none is invented here. A node with no operator-supplied peer
now has nothing to contact and says so. --seed-node, --add-peer,
--add-priority-node and --add-exclusive-node are unaffected.

NO UPDATE SUPPLY CHAIN

--check-updates defaulted to "notify" and the timer fired on the first idle
tick, so an ordinary start queried updates.moneropulse.* within seconds;
"download"/"update" would fetch a getmonero.org binary. The default is now
"disabled", any other value is refused at argument validation rather than
silently ignored, the periodic check is not scheduled, core::check_updates() is
a no-op, and the shared tools::check_updates()/get_update_url() fail closed with
cleared outputs and no URL. The daemon update RPC answers locally. Because the
helper is shared, the wallet API returns no update and no URL too. Endpoint
literals are deleted so their absence is visible in a built binary.

NO DNS BLOCKLIST AUTHORITY

Removed the seven blocklist.moneropulse.* endpoints. --enable-dns-blocklist
fails startup validation: accepting it and doing nothing would leave an operator
believing a protection was active. Operator banning (--ban-list, ban RPC) is
unaffected.

NO UNSOLICITED RESOLVER PROBE

The DNSResolver constructor probed updates.moneropulse.org the first time any
resolver was built -- including for a user-requested OpenAlias lookup -- and on
failure silently swapped the system resolver for hard-coded public ones. Both
are gone. Explicit DNS_PUBLIC and generic DNS/OpenAlias still work; this is not
a general DNS rewrite.

Also corrected one now-unreachable checkpoint warning that still named
MoneroPulse and --enforce-dns-checkpointing, neither of which this daemon has.

TESTS

  meepcoin-runtime-quarantine-test        45 passed, 0 failed
  meepcoin_runtime_quarantine_guards.sh   81 passed, 0 failed
  meepcoin-trust-anchor-test              66 passed, 0 failed (unchanged)
  meepcoin_trust_anchor_guards.sh         24 passed, 0 failed (unchanged)

Neither new test makes a DNS, TCP or HTTP request. Peer/blocklist/resolver
removals are asserted against the source and the built binary rather than by
instantiating node_server<> or constructing a resolver and hoping nothing
leaves the machine.

OUT OF SCOPE, DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED

Genesis values, network UUIDs, ports, address prefixes, emission, difficulty,
timestamp rules, hardforks, MeepHash sources and wallet key formats are
byte-identical. Wallet donation branding, research citations, hardware-wallet
names, cryptographic domain separators and the debug-only DNS utility are left
alone; they are separate design decisions, not part of this change.

